        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these treadmills. They were chosen from manufacturer specification
            tables, retailer listings, warranty terms and independent hands-on reviews, checked on 15
            October 2026, and the claims on this page are limited to what those sources support.
            Several have their own review pages on this site, which are readings of the published
            evidence rather than hands-on tests.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four rules decided the list. <strong>Currently sold:</strong> each machine had to be on
            sale under its current name on the maker's own store on the day we checked.
            <strong>Width confirmed twice:</strong> the maker's own specification had to give a
            22-inch running surface, and at least one independent source — a hands-on review or a
            second retailer — had to agree. <strong>A spread of prices:</strong> we wanted a pick at
            each step from about $1,400 to a commercial-grade machine. <strong>No open safety
            action:</strong> we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall
            database for every brand.
          </p>
          <p>
            Several popular machines failed the width rule, and each failure is instructive. Sole's
            current page lists the <strong>F80</strong> with a 20 by 60-inch deck, while many
            reviews of earlier F80s describe 22 inches; Sole has added a new
            <strong>F83</strong> with a 22-inch deck, but we found no independent coverage of the
            2026 version yet. NordicTrack's current page lists the <strong>Commercial 1250 and
            1750</strong> at 22 inches wide, but older listings for the 1250 give 20 inches, and one
            independent test that measured a Commercial-series belt found about 21.5 inches. Those
            machines may be perfectly good; we could not confirm their width for the model year on
            sale, so they are not on a page about width. Our
            <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-vs-1750/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Commercial 1250 vs 1750 comparison</a>
            covers them in detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            On safety, the recall database showed no recall covering any of the six machines here.
            The treadmill recalls we found for these makers concern other models: Horizon's T101-05
            folding treadmill in 2022, for unexpected speed changes, over which its parent company
            agreed in August 2026 to pay a civil penalty for late reporting; Nautilus treadmills
            with model numbers T616 and T618 in 2022; and Johnson Health Tech's Matrix and Vision
            treadmills for a power-cord fire hazard, expanded in January 2026. <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Life Fitness</a>'s only
            treadmill recall we found dates from 2003 and covered other models. If you are buying
            secondhand, check the model and serial number on cpsc.gov yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="usable-width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Listed Width Versus Usable Width</h2>
<p>
            The width on a listing is a measurement of something, but not always of the thing your
            feet use. Three different numbers get called "belt width", and they can differ by an
            inch or more.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Belt width, running surface and the space between the rails</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt width</strong> is the width of the belt itself, edge to edge.
            <strong>Running surface</strong> or running area is what most makers quote, and it is
            usually the belt width or very close to it. <strong>Usable width</strong> is the flat,
            exposed belt between the side rails or foot rails, and that is the number that decides
            whether a slightly wide step lands on moving belt or on a stationary rail. On many
            treadmills the rails sit over the outer edge of the belt, so the exposed width is a
            little less than the quoted width.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is not a theoretical gap. One independent reviewer that measures belts found the
            Horizon 7.4 AT's running surface matched Horizon's 22-inch claim, and measured a
            NordicTrack Commercial-series belt quoted at 22 inches at about 21.5. Half an inch is
            not a reason to reject a machine, but it is a reason not to treat two 22-inch listings as
            identical, and a reason to be wary of any listing that gives a width to the tenth of an
            inch without saying what was measured.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to check width before you buy</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Read the maker's own specification table</strong>, not a retailer title. Titles are the most common source of wrong widths.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the model year.</strong> Several makers have changed deck widths between years while keeping the model name.</li>
            <li><strong>Look for an independent measurement.</strong> A review that put a tape measure across the belt is worth more than any number of spec tables that agree because they copied each other.</li>
            <li><strong>If you can, stand on one.</strong> A showroom floor model tells you in seconds whether the rails crowd your stride.</li>
            <li><strong>Look at the rails, not just the belt.</strong> Low, flat foot rails give a wide step somewhere safe to land; tall or rounded side covers do not.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you already own a treadmill and want to know what you have, measure the exposed belt
            between the rails at the middle of the deck with the machine switched off and unplugged.
            That is your usable width, whatever the box said.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="width-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. A Five-Minute Width Test</h2>
<p>
            If you can get to a showroom, a sports retailer or a gym with a similar machine, five
            minutes on a belt tells you more about width than any amount of reading. You do not need
            the exact model; you need a 20-inch belt and a 22-inch belt to compare.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Walk first, at your normal pace, without holding on.</strong> Notice where your feet land relative to the centre line. Most people find a 20-inch belt fine at a walk, which is useful information in itself.</li>
            <li><strong>Jog at an easy pace for two minutes.</strong> Look down once, briefly, and see how much belt is left on each side of your shoes. If you are already close to a rail at an easy pace, width is likely to matter to you.</li>
            <li><strong>Turn your head toward a screen or a wall for thirty seconds.</strong> This is the drift most people do at home without noticing. See whether your feet follow your head.</li>
            <li><strong>Swing your arms naturally.</strong> If your hands or elbows brush the side covers or handrails, the problem is the frame as much as the belt, and the rail layout matters when you compare machines.</li>
            <li><strong>Repeat on the other width.</strong> If you cannot feel a difference, save the money.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Keep the safety key clipped on throughout, start every speed change from a pace you are
            comfortable with, and stop if anything feels unstable. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety tips</a>
            cover the basics for an unfamiliar machine. If you cannot try one, the next best thing
            is a published review whose tester describes their own build and stride, because a
            reviewer of a similar size to you is a better guide than a spec table.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-needs-width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Who Actually Needs a 22-Inch Belt</h2>
<p>
            Most home treadmills have 20-inch belts, and most people walk and run on them without a
            problem. The extra two inches earn their cost for a specific set of users, and it is
            worth being honest about whether you are one of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Runners who drift when tired.</strong> At an easy pace most people hold a line
            down the centre of the belt. Late in a long run or in the last intervals of a hard
            session, foot placement wanders, and a runner who drifts an inch or two to one side has
            more margin on a 22-inch belt before a foot clips the rail. This is the most common
            reason runners give for wanting width, and it is a good one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Broad-shouldered and larger runners.</strong> Width is not only about feet. A
            broad frame and a natural arm swing carry the hands and elbows out to the sides, and on a
            narrow machine with high side covers or handrails that can feel cramped even when the
            feet are fine. Heavier users also tend to run with a slightly wider stance. If you are
            shopping for that reason, read our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for heavy people</a>
            alongside this page, because the weight rating, frame and motor matter at least as much
            as the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Runners with a wide natural step.</strong> People differ in step width — how
            far apart the feet land side to side. A runner whose feet land well apart uses more of
            the belt than one whose feet land close to a single line. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-running-form/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill running form</a>
            explains how to notice your own pattern; this page will not tell you to change it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anyone who watches a screen while running.</strong> Turning your head toward a
            television or a side-mounted tablet tends to pull the body with it. A wider belt is more
            forgiving of that drift.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Tall runners, sometimes.</strong> Height mainly calls for belt length, not width;
            our page on the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for tall runners</a>
            covers that. But tall runners are often broad and long-limbed too, and many of the
            machines with 60-inch decks that suit them happen to be 22 inches wide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="model-years" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Why Width Figures Conflict So Often</h2>
<p>
            While putting this page together, the width figure for more than one well-known model
            was different depending on where we looked. Our own pages on
            <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack</a>
            and
            <a href="/best-sole-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a>
            treadmills ran into the same thing. There are four usual causes.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>The model changed and the name did not.</strong> Treadmill makers update machines every year or two and often keep the name. A deck that was 20 inches in one year can be 22 in the next, or the reverse, and old reviews and old stock stay online.</li>
            <li><strong>Retailer listings are copied.</strong> A wrong figure in one marketplace title tends to propagate, so ten listings agreeing is not ten independent confirmations.</li>
            <li><strong>Different things are measured.</strong> Belt, running surface and usable width can each be called "width", as above.</li>
            <li><strong>Regional versions differ.</strong> The same model name can be sold with different specifications in Europe and North America; a metric figure from a European listing is not always the US machine converted.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            The practical consequence is simple. When a listing and the maker's current page
            disagree, trust the maker's current page for the model year you are buying, and ask the
            seller to confirm the model year in writing. When a discounted listing quotes a wider
            deck than the maker does, assume it may be older stock until you know otherwise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="room-and-frame" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. A Wider Belt Means a Bigger Machine</h2>
<p>
            Two inches of belt usually adds more than two inches of machine. Wider decks come on
            heavier frames with bigger motors, and the footprints of the six machines here are
            noticeably larger than those of typical 20-inch folders. Before buying, check four
            things.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Floor space.</strong> Use the assembled length and width from the maker's
            specification, then add the clearance behind and to the sides that safe use needs. Our
            guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill dimensions and space requirements</a>
            gives the margins.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Doorways and stairs.</strong> A wide, heavy treadmill arrives in a large box and
            is assembled in the room. Measure the narrowest doorway and the turn at the top of any
            stairs against the box size, not the assembled machine, and consider paying for
            room-of-choice delivery.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ceiling height.</strong> Step-up height plus your own height plus a margin for
            the bounce of running decides whether the ceiling is close. Machines with a lower deck,
            such as the <a href="/life-fitness-run-cx-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Life Fitness Run CX</a>, help in a basement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Folding.</strong> Five of the six machines here fold, but folding a heavy
            22-inch deck is not like folding a small machine: it still takes floor space when
            folded and it is not something to move from room to room. If you want a machine that
            stays put, our page on the
            <a href="/best-non-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best non-folding treadmills</a>
            covers the case for a fixed frame.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="beyond-width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Width Is Not the Only Specification</h2>
<p>
            A wide belt on a machine that is wrong in every other way is not a good buy. Once width
            is settled, check these in roughly this order.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Length.</strong> Every machine here is 60 inches long, which is the usual
            recommendation for running. A wide but short deck — common on walking-focused machines —
            solves the wrong problem for a runner.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Maximum user weight.</strong> If you are buying width because you are a larger
            runner, the weight rating matters at least as much. The ratings on this page run from
            350 to 400 lb where sources agree; two machines have ratings that differ between
            sources and are discussed on their cards. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            explains what the rating does and does not tell you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor.</strong> Motor ratings were the figure sources disagreed on most often,
            which is why most of them are missing from the cards. A wider, heavier belt asks more of
            the motor at every speed. For walking and steady running, any machine on this page is
            likely to be adequate; for heavy daily use by a heavy runner, prefer a machine with a
            long motor warranty, which every pick here has.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck and cushioning.</strong> A wider belt sits on a wider deck, and the deck
            and its cushioning decide how the machine feels underfoot. Every pick here describes a
            cushioned deck; none of the makers publishes a figure that can be compared across
            brands, so treat percentages in marketing copy with caution and read what independent
            reviewers say about firmness. Our guide to
            <a href="/best-cushioned-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cushioned treadmills</a>
            goes into how the systems differ.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step-up height.</strong> Wide, heavy decks often sit higher off the floor. A
            higher deck is a bigger step on and off, and it takes from the ceiling height you have
            for running. If either matters, find the maker's step-up figure and confirm it with a
            review, because it is another number that is quoted differently by different sources.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Screen and subscriptions.</strong> Three of these machines are designed around
            an app ecosystem. None requires a subscription to run the treadmill itself, but the
            experience the maker advertises often does. Check the current price of any subscription
            before you factor it in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you mainly walk</strong>, a 20-inch belt is almost certainly enough. Walking
            keeps the feet close to a line, and the money saved on a narrower machine is better spent
            on length, cushioning or a better warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your budget is under about $1,400</strong>, the cheapest 22-inch machine we
            could verify was the Bowflex T9 on sale. Below that, listings that claim 22 inches tend
            to be older stock or figures we could not confirm. A good 20-inch machine is a better
            buy than an unverified 22-inch one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your room is small</strong>, the frame size of a wide treadmill may be the
            deciding factor, and a 20-inch folder that fits the room is better than a 22-inch
            machine that blocks it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are buying a walking pad</strong>, width works differently: pads usually
            have belts around 15 to 20 inches wide, and they are not meant for running at all. Our
            <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>
            covers what to look for instead.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have balance concerns or a medical condition</strong> that makes you
            wonder about width, that is a question for a qualified professional, not a
            specification sheet. A wider belt is more forgiving of drift; it is not a safety
            device.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best wide treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Horizon 7.4 AT: a 22 by 60-inch deck that an independent reviewer measured at full width, a 350 lb rating, 15% incline and lifetime frame and motor cover, for about $1,500 to $1,800. The Bowflex T9 is the cheapest 22-inch belt we could verify, and the Horizon 7.8 AT suits heavier runners.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 22 inch wide treadmill worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it if you run, drift sideways when tired, have a broad build or a wide natural step, or watch a screen while running. If you mainly walk, a 20-inch belt is usually enough and the money is better spent on length, cushioning or warranty. Wider treadmills are also bigger and heavier machines overall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 20 inch treadmill belt wide enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most walkers and many runners, yes. Twenty inches is the common home width and works for anyone who holds a fairly straight line. It starts to feel narrow at faster paces, late in long runs, for larger runners and for anyone whose feet land wide apart. If you can, stand and jog on one before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I measure the usable width of a treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With the treadmill switched off and unplugged, measure across the exposed belt between the side rails or foot rails, at the middle of the deck. That is the width your feet can use. It can be a little less than the listed belt width, because on many machines the rails sit over the outer edges of the belt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do heavier people need a wider treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often it helps, because a larger build and a wider stance use more of the belt, but width alone is not enough. A heavier user should check the maximum user weight, the frame, the motor warranty and the deck first, and then prefer a 22-inch belt among the machines that pass. Weight rating matters at least as much as width.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do treadmill width specs differ between websites?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually because the model changed between years while keeping its name, because a wrong retailer title was copied, or because different sources measured belt, running surface or the space between the rails. Trust the maker's current specification for the model year you are buying, and look for an independent review that measured the belt.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Wide Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these leaves a buyer with a narrower belt, a bigger machine or a bigger bill than they expected.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the width in a listing title</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Marketplace titles are the most common source of wrong widths, and they get copied from listing to listing. Check the maker's own current specification table for the exact model year, and look for an independent review that measured the belt. If a discounted listing claims a wider deck than the maker does, assume older stock until the seller confirms otherwise in writing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming 22 inches is all usable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">On many treadmills the side rails or covers sit over the edge of the belt, so the exposed width you can step on is smaller than the quoted figure. One independent test measured about half an inch less than claimed on a 22-inch machine. Look at the rails as well as the belt, and stand on a floor model if you can.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the machine around the belt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Wider decks come on wider, heavier frames. Buyers measure the floor for the belt and then find the machine blocks a walkway, will not fit through a doorway in its box, or is too heavy to move once built. Use the assembled dimensions and the box size from the maker, and add the clearance behind and beside the machine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for width you do not need</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">If you walk at a steady pace with your feet close to a line, a 20-inch belt is almost certainly enough, and a wide machine costs more, weighs more and takes more room. Buy width for running, drifting, a broad build or a wide step. For walking, spend the difference on length, cushioning or a longer warranty instead.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

